ectory and socket differences are normal reboot-generated identity/PID changes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9a1bdbdb45bf41ef","cmd-813787c995174230","global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied. Direct evidence shows audit=1 configured in all boot entries, a completed reboot with a different boot identity, audit=1 active afterward, kernel auditing enabled, and the original RHEL release and kernel retained. The provider-wide restart protocol is strong: it includes a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgement, expected connection interruption, changed post-restart identity, running recovered system, read-write root filesystem, readable OS/kernel files, and restored baseline sockets. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["No application-specific service, dataset, or cluster was identified for recovery testing; recovery evidence therefore covers the single node's system state, root filesystem, required OS/kernel files, and baseline network services."]}
